// @check
// @error no `foo` on `T` — its bound `Showable` grants `toString()`
// STRICT BODIES (OQ-5, ruled): inside `function f<T: B>(x: T)`, member use on `x`
// resolves against B's members ONLY.
//
// This is also the root fix for D952 — the wordless refusal. `x.foo` on a declared
// type parameter used to print `[ERROR]: ` with a caret and NOTHING else, because
// `memberFloorMsg`'s `TyVar` rung returned the empty string and `tErrPush` does not
// guard against one. There was no wording to search for, quote or improve, and the
// author's likeliest next move — deleting the `<T>` — silently changes the
// program's meaning.
//
// A BOUNDED parameter has a real answer: the bound is named, and so is everything
// it grants, so the reader can see in one line whether the fix is to spell the
// member differently or to widen the bound. THIS IS THE SENTENCE A REAL BOUND GETS,
// and it is the register every neighbour was brought to. An UNBOUNDED `<T>` no
// longer refuses at all (D1431, `unbounded-member-no-bound.vl`), and an annotation
// that is not a bound gets its own words — `error-member-on-malformed-bound.vl`
// and `error-member-on-field-member-alias-bound.vl`.
type Showable = { toString(): string }

function bad<T: Showable>(x: T): string { x.foo }

print("unreached")
